#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125292} :activity/id #uuid "b218a927-38fe-5672-a4d0-f4c57476e22f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049511 :topic/id #uuid "fabe76f4-9f30-5b59-b906-f5fa71cfb89f" :topic/math-academy-id 1940} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125733 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the equation of the **tangent line** to the ellipse \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{12} + \\dfrac{y^2}{4} =1\n$$\n\n at the point $P(3,1).$\n\nTo solve this problem, we could find the slope of the line using techniques from single-variable calculus. However, we can also solve the problem by computing the gradient of a two-variable function.\n\nLet's start by defining \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}{12} + \\dfrac {y^2}{4}.\n$$\n\nThen, the given ellipse is the level curve  $f(x,y)=1.$\n\nNow, we know that $\\nabla \\! f(3,1)$ is a vector that's normal to the level curve $f(x,y)= 1$ at $P(3,1).$ So, it's also normal to the tangent line to our ellipse at $P.$\n\n![](images/3d/3dc75e8fd4083af1bf90f46b2e875d5d552bffdc5c1cfaeabebef70f79f00828.png)\n\nSuppose that $(x,y)$ is any point on that tangent line. Then $\\mathbf v=\\langle x - 3, y-1 \\rangle$ is a vector that's parallel to the tangent line. So $\\mathbf v$ must be also orthogonal to $\\nabla f(3,1),$ and since the dot product of two orthogonal vectors is zero, we have that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(3,1)\\,\\cdot \\,\\langle x - 3, y-1 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, computing $\\nabla\\! f$ and evaluating it at the point $P(3,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{x}{6},\\frac{y}{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,f(3,1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the equation of our tangent, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(3,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x-3)+\\frac{1}{2}(y-1) & =0 \\\\ (x-3)+(y-1) & =0 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7a9dede8-0672-56eb-9084-cf581e3fb05f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2496 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7cc9655e-83bb-5f85-8c20-add1d27fb5e0" :step/math-academy-id 11184 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125464}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125293 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124454 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124258 :question/id #uuid "a01560be-9023-593a-87d3-7412f3b208a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3362"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ae7e9c8-60e9-5f5e-afbf-1de6bb01bf07" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050027}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393208}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Line to a Level Curve"} :step/id #uuid "204027bd-d606-5131-82eb-717c2823cc14" :step/math-academy-id 11185 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125294}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125734 :tutorial/content "Given a level curve $f(x,y) = f(x_0,y_0),$ a **tangent vector** is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x_0,y_0) = f_y(x_0,y_0) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x_0,y_0) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo understand why the above vector is tangent to the level curve, first recall that the gradient vector $\\nabla f(x_0,y_0)$ is normal to the level curve. Now, by computing the dot product, we see that the vector $\\mathbf t$ is orthogonal to the gradient vector $\\nabla\\! f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f\\cdot t & =({f}_{x}\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}\\text{ }j)\\cdot ({f}_{y}\\text{ }i-{f}_{x}j) \\\\  & ={f}_{x}{f}_{y}-{f}_{y}{f}_{x} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf t$ is tangent to the level curve." :tutorial/id #uuid "6ef07808-783a-543e-9217-5d3d0a9ae13a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2497 :tutorial/title "The Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"} :step/id #uuid "6501f944-f162-5a6e-9f8e-4ccc2cd2ce41" :step/math-academy-id 11188 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124455 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124259 :question/id #uuid "f6400ab7-3684-5f0d-b8f0-1c85b0b322ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-3363"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339122} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373750}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"} :step/id #uuid "1ce011ea-75f6-5023-9ba5-8deb530c69ba" :step/math-academy-id 11189 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125296}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124456 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124260 :question/id #uuid "c4ca2e8a-5a5b-54c6-983d-2f5e821084ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-3364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403571}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"} :step/id #uuid "6e185929-7cee-53dd-9616-e86a123783e5" :step/math-academy-id 11192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125807 :tutorial/content "Now, let's state the previous result more generally.\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be a function that's continuously differentiable at the point $P(x_0,y_0).$   If  $\\nabla f(x_0, y_0)$ is not the zero vector, then it is orthogonal to the tangent line to the level curve \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= f(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nat the point $P.$ The equation of the tangent can then be written as\n\n$$\n\\nabla\\! f (x_0,y_0) \\cdot \\langle x-x_0,y-y_0\\rangle  = 0.\n$$\n\nWriting this explicitly, we get\n\n$$\nf_x(x_0,y_0)(x-x_0) + f_y(x_0,y_0)(y-y_0)   = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e9c2ee0b-08cf-5f42-bf09-f80dcd82051d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3866 :tutorial/title "Tangent Line to a Level Curve"} :step/id #uuid "2e3c389c-2b5c-55ca-92a8-e52fcbcf9f84" :step/math-academy-id 22698 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125293}}] :activity/title "Tangent Lines to Level Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124732}]}
